{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The report stream, and where a failure goes - Design-Test.md §5.1 and §5.2.
   ==========================================================================================

   One line per event, textual, written as it happens:

       begin 17 Map.duplicate_insert
       fail  17 Map.test:41:5  expected 1, got 2
       end   17 fail 214000

   **Line-oriented and unbuffered, for one reason:** a case that takes the process down loses one
   line rather than the whole report. A binary framing would lose everything after the truncation,
   and losing the report of a run that crashed is losing the only thing worth having. It is also
   what makes crash attribution free - §5.3's middle tier is a `begin` with no `end`, and naming the
   case that died needs no second process, no ptrace and no debugger.

   **The recorder is a global rather than a parameter**, which is §5.1's ruling. Threading a
   `&Report` through every assertion would put it in every *helper's* signature too, and the
   exclusivity rule then forbids a helper holding that borrow across a nested call that also wants
   it. There is one runner in a process, so nothing is being hidden.

   And the state is scalars, which is not a preference: a global's initializer has to have a
   constant form, so `let &failures = [] :: [Failure]` does not compile in a library module - only
   the root's top level may hold one. That turns out to be the better shape anyway. A failure is
   *written* where it happens rather than accumulated and printed at the end, which is exactly what
   makes the stream survive a case that never returns.
-}

import Native

-- Which case is being reported, and how many claims did not hold - the whole of the runner's state.
-- `caseFailures` is reset per case and `totalFailures` is not, so the summary and the per-case
-- verdict are two counters rather than one counter and a list.
let &ordinal = 0 :: Int
let &caseFailures = 0 :: Int
let &totalFailures = 0 :: Int
let &caseName = "" :: String

{-
   The same failures, in the shape a person reads - Design-Test.md §5.5.

   Not a second record of what happened: it is the *claims* of the case being run, without the
   ordinal and the `fail` keyword the stream frames them with, kept because `Test.Console` gathers a
   run's failures and prints them under the summary rather than as they happen. The stream must do
   the opposite - write each one at the moment it happens, so that a case which never returns has
   still said what went wrong - and the two shapes are what the two readers need.

   A `String` global, which a library module may have because `""` is a constant initializer -
   Section.yana's `sectionLabels` is the same shape for the same reason. It costs one string per
   *failing* case: a case that holds appends nothing here, and a run with no console reads it never.
-}
let &caseDetail = "" :: String

{-
   Whether the stream has been handed over to something that reports better - Design-Test.md §5.5.

   The native stream goes to descriptor 3 when a parent opened one and to standard error otherwise,
   and the second half is what this is about. Standard error is *also* where a person watching the
   run is looking, so a binary run at a terminal wrote `begin 3 Map.insert` into the middle of the
   line the console was rewriting - two reports of the same events, interleaved, one of them for a
   reader that is not there.

   So a console that is drawing on a terminal mutes it, and what it replaces is not nothing: the
   property the stream buys is that a case which takes the process down still names itself, and the
   progress line names the case that is running and stays on the screen when the process dies. Same
   guarantee, one reader.

   **Muting is refused while the stream is private**, which is the whole safety of it: descriptor 3
   being open means something is reading, and a run that silenced a channel somebody was reading
   would turn a crashed child into one that reported nothing at all. A run with no terminal - a
   pipe, a redirect, a CI log - is not muted either, because there is no progress line there to have
   replaced it.
-}
let &muted = False

pub fn muteReport() -> {}:
    if reportIsPrivate() then return
    muted = True

pub fn reportMuted() -> Bool = muted

{-
   One claim that did not hold, written to the stream and counted.

   Public, because a user-written assertion is an ordinary function that ends in this - which is what
   §4.3's "every assertion in the library has this shape" means in practice. The `check…` family
   below is nothing but a condition and a call to this.
-}
pub fn record(failure: Failure) -> {}:
    recordAt(failure.site, failure.claim, failure.detail)

{-
   The same, from the pieces rather than from the record.

   The harness needs this: a case it ran in a child has a verdict and a `Site` that belongs to the
   `Case`, and building a `Failure` out of them would mean *owning* copies of two strings that are
   already there. Nothing about a report line needs the record - it needs four fields to read.
-}
pub fn recordAt(site: Site, claim: String, detail: String) -> {}:
    caseFailures = caseFailures + 1
    totalFailures = totalFailures + 1

    -- Built once and framed twice: the stream prefixes the ordinal so that a parent can tie the line
    -- to a `begin`, and the console does not because it is already under the name of the case.
    let &claimed = "{site.file}:{site.line}:{site.column}"
    if length(sectionLabels) > (0 :: Size) then claimed ++= "  [{sectionLabels}]"
    claimed ++= "  {claim}"

    let &line = "fail  {ordinal} {claimed}\n"
    if length(detail) > (0 :: Size) then line ++= "      {detail}\n"

    caseDetail ++= "{claimed}\n"
    if length(detail) > (0 :: Size) then caseDetail ++= "  {detail}\n"

    reportLine(line)

-- How many claims the case being run has already lost. What `require…` reads to decide whether it
-- has anything to add, and what the runner reads to decide the verdict.
pub fn currentFailures() -> Int = caseFailures

pub fn totalFailureCount() -> Int = totalFailures

{-
   The two lines that bracket a case.

   `begin` carries the ordinal and the qualified name; `end` carries the verdict and how long the
   case took, in nanoseconds. The ordinal is what ties the three line kinds together and is what a
   parent reads: a `begin` whose `end` never arrived names the case that crashed.
-}
pub fn beginCase(index: Int, name: String) -> {}:
    ordinal = index
    caseFailures = 0
    caseName = copy(name)
    caseDetail = ""
    reportLine("begin {index} {name}\n")

pub fn endCase(verdict: String, nanos: I64) -> {}:
    reportLine("end   {ordinal} {verdict} {bitcast(nanos) :: Long}\n")

-- The name of the case being run, for a report written from outside the case's own body - which is
-- the harness reporting a child that never came back.
pub fn currentCase() -> String = copy(caseName)

-- What the case being run has lost so far, as the lines a person reads. Empty for a case that has
-- held, which is what lets a reporter ask without first asking whether there is anything to ask for.
pub fn currentDetail() -> String = copy(caseDetail)
